<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of East Florida</h1><p class="st-meta">1763 CE – 1783 CE · capital St. Augustine</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived British colony on the Florida peninsula, governed from St. Augustine and loyal to the Crown throughout the American Revolution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> received <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763, in exchange for returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. The Proclamation of 1763 split the territory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apalachicola-river">Apalachicola River</a>, creating East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> with its capital at the old Spanish town of St. Augustine. Almost the entire Spanish population left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Royal governors, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-grant-british-army-officer">James Grant</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrick-tonyn">Patrick Tonyn</a>, tried to attract settlers with land grants. The most ambitious scheme, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-turnbull">Andrew Turnbull's</a> New <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/smyrna">Smyrna</a> colony of Minorcans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and Italians, collapsed amid disease and hardship. Plantations produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and naval stores. The province did not join the rebellion of 1776 and became a refuge for thousands of Loyalists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> and the Carolinas.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1783 returned both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Floridas</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Most British settlers and Loyalists departed for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> or <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, and the territory became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a> again until its cession to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in 1821.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-east-florida.jpg" alt="Province of East Florida" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Wangi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_ensign_of_Great_Britain_(1707%E2%80%931800).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1763 CE – 1783 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>St. Augustine</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>James Grant, Patrick Tonyn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>East Florida</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1773&amp;focus=province-of-east-florida" aria-label="Province of East Florida on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2566 1086 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of East Florida lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2886 1342L2886 1342M2886 1342L4997 651"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2886 1342h0M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2886 1342h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2886 1342h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks St. Augustine. <a href="/atlas?year=1773&amp;focus=province-of-east-florida">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
